**定义 76.8（full query and finite output framing）。** A query is the literal symbol Q, followed by a nonempty canonical binary expansion of $j$, followed by a semicolon and then end-of-input. Its domain is $j\geq3$. A multi-digit expansion cannot start with zero. Negative signs, nonbinary symbols, empty or truncated frames, extra semicolons, a missing leading Q and all trailing tokens are invalid. Even after saturation or discovery of an error, the parser consumes every remaining input character through end-of-input. Finite input is an interface promise; an infinite stream has no asserted termination.

For a loss word the parser maintains a value saturated at $J$ and a residue modulo three, together with framing, first-digit, seen-digit and invalidity flags. At a binary digit $d$, update the value to $\min(J,2v+d)$ and the residue to $(2r+d)\bmod3$. Thus it preserves the actual phase after saturation without retaining the unbounded integer $j$. For periodic, orientation and zero words the cutoff can be three; the same residue and validity rules apply. A malformed word still causes the entire finite query to be consumed before an error is returned. The transport may remove one final newline using one-character lookahead; it consumes that character and all subsequent bytes before end-of-input. An additional newline is a trailing-token error. This transport convention supplies no early-return shortcut.

A dyadic vector output is a leading V, gamma of its precision, three gamma-coded signed numerators and a final semicolon. A rational vector output is a leading R, three signed-numerator/positive-denominator field pairs and a final semicolon. An error output is the four-symbol constant ERR;. There are no implicit coordinates, precision fields or output bytes. A rational periodic output is reduced before these fields are written. Finite mode, word and query parsing, all setup, every query character, arithmetic and complete output transmission count in the resource vector of §85. Repeated queries may reuse an installed finite state; invoking a fresh decoder pays setup again.

